# Flow Language

The Flow language declares a graph of named Action calls and one output. It is
a compile-time Spark DSL. Its expressions become data. They do not run
application code while the module compiles.

The DSL, `Jido.Flow.Builder`, and stored JSON all produce the same canonical
`%Jido.Flow{}`. The DSL is the primary developer authoring surface.

Run the code cells from top to bottom.

## Install Jido Action

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0.0-rc.1"}])
```

## Define Small Actions

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.Echo do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_language_echo",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(params, _context), do: {:ok, params}
end

defmodule FlowLanguage.Double do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_language_double",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{value: value}, _context), do: {:ok, %{value: value * 2}}
end

defmodule FlowLanguage.Add do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_language_add",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{total: Zoi.integer(), value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{total: Zoi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{total: total, value: value}, _context), do: {:ok, %{total: total + value}}
end
```

## `step`: Call An Action

`step` declares one named Action or nested Flow call. `params:` maps Flow data
to target input. The last node becomes the output when `output` is absent.

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.StepExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_language_step",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "echo",
      action: FlowLanguage.Echo,
      params: %{value: input(:value)}
  end
end

{:ok, %{value: 7}} = Jido.Exec.run(FlowLanguage.StepExample, %{value: 7}, %{})
```

## Independent Nodes Are Implicitly Parallel

References define graph dependencies. These two root nodes are independent.
The final node waits for both because it reads both results.

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.ParallelExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_language_parallel",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{left: Zoi.integer(), right: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "left",
      action: FlowLanguage.Echo,
      params: %{value: input(:left)}

    step "right",
      action: FlowLanguage.Echo,
      params: %{value: input(:right)}

    step "merge",
      action: FlowLanguage.Echo,
      params: %{
        left: select(result("left"), :value),
        right: select(result("right"), :value)
      }
  end
end

{:ok, %{left: 1, right: 2}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(
    FlowLanguage.ParallelExample,
    %{left: 1, right: 2},
    %{},
    async: true,
    max_concurrency: 2
  )
```

Source order does not add an edge. Use `after:` when you need control ordering
without a data reference.

## `map` And `reduce`: Process Collections

`map` calls one target for each item. `reduce` performs a serial left fold.
Both have short and block forms.

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.CollectionExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_language_collections",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{values: Zoi.list(Zoi.integer())}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    map "double_values",
      collection: input(:values),
      action: FlowLanguage.Double,
      params: %{value: item()}

    reduce "sum_values" do
      collection(result("double_values"))
      initial(%{total: 0})
      action(FlowLanguage.Add)
      params(%{total: accumulator(:total), value: item(:value)})
    end
  end
end

{:ok, %{total: 12}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowLanguage.CollectionExample, %{values: [1, 2, 3]}, %{})
```

Map produces an aggregate. Reduce can consume that complete aggregate and read
its ordered successful outputs.

## `iterate`: Repeat Bounded Work

`iterate` owns a State schema and initial State. It runs one target through
either a fixed `repeat` count or a bounded `while` condition. If `update` is
absent, the complete Action result becomes the next State.

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.IterateExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_language_iterate",
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    iterate "add_twice" do
      state([], initial: %{total: 0})
      action(FlowLanguage.Add)
      params(%{total: state(:total), value: 1})
      repeat(2)
    end
  end
end

{:ok, %{iterations: 2, state: %{total: 2}, output: %{total: 2}}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowLanguage.IterateExample, %{}, %{})
```

See [Iterate and State](flow-iterate-state.livemd) for State updates and `while`.

## `choice`: Select One Target

`choice` checks options in source order. The first true native Elixir-style
condition selects a target. The required `otherwise` target is the fallback.

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.ChoiceExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_language_choice",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{kind: Zoi.atom()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    choice "route" do
      option "special",
        condition: input(:kind) == :special,
        action: FlowLanguage.Echo,
        params: %{route: :special}

      otherwise action: FlowLanguage.Echo,
                params: %{route: :default}
    end
  end
end

{:ok, %{route: :special}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowLanguage.ChoiceExample, %{kind: :special}, %{})

{:ok, %{route: :default}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowLanguage.ChoiceExample, %{kind: :other}, %{})
```

## Expressions And Explicit Output

Expressions can read `input`, `context`, and earlier `result` values. `select`
projects a nested path. Literals, maps, and lists stay data. Use `output` to
shape a value from multiple sources.

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.ExpressionExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_language_expressions",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "source",
      action: FlowLanguage.Echo,
      params: %{value: input(:value), constant: :constant}

    output %{
      value: select(result("source"), :value),
      trace_id: context(:trace_id),
      constant: select(result("source"), :constant)
    }
  end
end

{:ok, %{value: 7, trace_id: "trace-1", constant: :constant}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowLanguage.ExpressionExample, %{value: 7}, %{trace_id: "trace-1"})
```

The expression surface is closed. Put computation in an Action. Do not call
application functions inside `params`, `condition`, State, or `output`.

## One Canonical Flow Artifact

```elixir
canonical = FlowLanguage.StepExample.flow()

true = is_struct(canonical, Jido.Flow)
%Jido.Flow{name: "flow_language_step", nodes: [_node], return: _output} = canonical

{:ok, identity} = Jido.Flow.semantic_identity(canonical)
true = is_binary(identity.digest)
```

